    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "# Female GT vs. Round7 Refractive Track"
      },
      {
        "id": "technical_summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Technical summary\n\n**The human female labels are internally strong enough to show that the round7 refractive geometry IDs are not stable biological identities.** Of 298 mapped confirmed labels, the best single ID (`ID_1`) covers only 43.3%; the annotation sequence contains at least 26 changes across 27 observed runs. A 'change' means adjacent mapped GT samples carry different geometry IDs, so it is a lower bound rather than an exact count of swap events.\n\n**Overall assessment: share the identity-inconsistency conclusion with caveats; revise the GT provenance before treating this ledger as a frozen gold release.** The ledger uses the placeholder annotator `YOUR_NAME`, and the saved candidate queue records an 80 px association gate although the supplied command states 8 px."
      },
      {
        "id": "headline_metrics",
        "type": "metric-strip",
        "cardIds": [
          "mapping_coverage_card",
          "fixed_id_card",
          "switch_card"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "identity_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## All three geometry IDs carry the female label\n\nThe mapped female labels split across `ID_1` (129), `ID_2` (68), and `ID_3` (101). A global `fish_id -> sex` lookup would therefore mislabel 56.7% of these mapped female anchors even under the best fixed choice. The implication is operational: sex must be attached after identity stitching, not directly to the current geometry ID."
      },
      {
        "id": "identity_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "identity_counts_chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "temporal_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## The dominant label shifts over recording time\n\n`ID_3` carries 68.4% of mapped female labels in the early candidate bin, whereas `ID_1` carries 55.3% in the middle and 64.8% in the late bin. This is temporal reassignment, not a small number of isolated row errors. The two manual-jump rows have no temporal-bin metadata and are excluded from this chart."
      },
      {
        "id": "temporal_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "temporal_identity_chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "sensitivity_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Strict 8 px filtering leaves the conclusion unchanged\n\nThe strict two-view subset retains 265 rows, 25 observed changes, and a best fixed-ID accuracy of only 44.2%. Adding the candidate-wide <=8 px rule retains 249 rows and 23 changes. This robustness check rules out the saved 80 px queue gate as the main explanation for the ID reassignment."
      },
      {
        "id": "sensitivity_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "sensitivity_chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "mapping_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Cross-view annotations are mostly self-consistent\n\nThe two side cameras agree on geometry ID for 283 of 300 confirmed rows (94.3%); 15 rows use one view and 2 disagree. All 298 selected consensus coordinates are finite and non-interpolated in the current track. This supports using the ledger as sparse identity evidence while keeping the disagreement and wide-gate rows out of a locked training set until re-reviewed."
      },
      {
        "id": "mapping_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "mapping_status_chart",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "scope_definitions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Scope, data, and metric definitions\n\nThe ledger contains 301 unique source frames from 4,439 to 348,388 at 166 fps: 300 confirmed female rows and 1 ambiguous row. The compared track is the current refractive output with 1,045,800 rows, frames 0-348,599, IDs `ID_1`-`ID_3`, and score unit `mm^2`. 'Best fixed-ID accuracy' is the largest female-label count for any one geometry ID divided by all mapped confirmed labels."
      },
      {
        "id": "methodology",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Exact joins and reprojection reproduce the GUI evidence\n\nThe audit validates the canonical `(experiment, frame, annotator)` key and deterministic annotation IDs, reconciles the append-only audit ledger, joins every selected geometry ID to the exact 3D frame, and reprojects that 3D point with the checked-in fixed-interface refractive calibration. It also rebuilds provisional tracklets with the production finite, non-interpolated, <=8 mm local-jump rule. The current provenance JSON hash and candidate CSV hash match the saved candidate-build records, and the track size matches the recorded source."
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Limitations and rows that need re-review\n\n**The 26 observed changes are a lower bound.** Sparse candidate spacing cannot identify the exact swap frame and can miss an even number of swaps that return to the same label. The reconstruction has no >8 mm consecutive non-interpolated jump, so smooth trajectory continuity alone does not protect biological identity at crossings.\n\n**The saved evidence is incompatible with an 8 px gate on every row.** The queue summary records 80 px, 32/300 candidate rows exceed 8 px at the candidate-wide maximum, and the selected-ID reprojection has a 74.4 px side2 outlier at frame 246665. These rows do not drive the conclusion, but they should not enter a frozen GT set without visual review."
      },
      {
        "id": "mapping_issue_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "mapping_issue_table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "transition_note",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Transition brackets localize identity-stitching work\n\nThe table lists every consecutive mapped GT pair whose geometry ID changes. Use each left/right bracket to inspect the full-resolution trajectory and side-camera evidence, then place a biological identity boundary or stitching constraint inside the interval."
      },
      {
        "id": "transition_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "transition_table",
        "layout": "full"
      },
      {
        "id": "tracklet_note",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Provisional tracklet joins are otherwise exact\n\nAll 296 non-empty saved tracklet IDs match the clean segment recomputed at the exact annotation frame. Two mapped manual-jump rows have no saved provisional tracklet; their recomputed exact-frame segments are clean."
      },
      {
        "id": "missing_tracklet_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "missing_tracklet_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "next_steps",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Recommended next steps\n\n1. Correct the placeholder annotator value through a controlled ledger migration that preserves the old audit records and regenerates deterministic annotation IDs.\n2. Re-open the two cross-view disagreement frames (290906 and 338780), the 74.4 px outlier at frame 246665, and the remaining rows in the re-review table with an explicit 8 px gate.\n3. Use the 26 transition brackets as sparse identity-stitching supervision; do not assign female sex from one global geometry ID.\n4. After stitching, rerun this audit and require one stable final female ID across the high-confidence subset."
      },
      {
        "id": "further_questions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Further questions\n\n- Which exact frames inside the 26 brackets contain the identity exchanges?\n- Do top-camera 2D identity anchors or segmentation identities collapse these runs to one biological female?\n- Should the two manual-jump records be re-saved from the candidate queue so they carry explicit provisional tracklet IDs?"
      }
    ]
  },
